{
  "version": "cc-sas-v1",
  "frozenAt": "E2E-P0 (design freeze). Changing any value here is a breaking protocol change and requires a new info string.",
  "info": "cc-sas-v1",
  "hash": "SHA-256",
  "curve": "P-256 (secp256r1/prime256v1). Gate 1 R1 2026-09-17: X25519 WITHDRAWN as the wire curve - AndroidKeyStore cannot hold it on any current release. Public keys are uncompressed SEC1 points: exactly 65 bytes, first byte 0x04, base64url on the wire and hex in this file. The SAS layout is unchanged; the epk and every key are simply 65 bytes long now (see v6-3key-p256).",
  "kdf": "HKDF (RFC 5869), salt = UTF8(pairingId), info = \"cc-sas-v1\"",
  "digits": "be32(OKM[0..4]) mod 100000, zero-padded to 5",
  "layout": [
    "0x01 || u8(len(epk))  || epk",
    "0x02 || u8(n)         || for each K in the sorted, deduplicated key SET: u8(len(K)) || K",
    "0x03 || be64(pairEpoch)",
    "0x04 || modeByte      (0x01 if EITHER side advertises Encrypted mode ON, else 0x00)"
  ],
  "keySetRule": "ALL static public keys in the pairing - the phone plus every recipient (web, service worker, further computers) - deduplicated and sorted by UNSIGNED lexicographic byte comparison. One code per pairing, never one per recipient: under a per-recipient SAS the service worker has no UI to display its code on, so a swapped SW key would be invisible to the user while remaining the leg that decrypts notification bodies with the panel closed (AUDIT-SECURITY-v2 B9, correcting v1 B7).",
  "modeByteRule": "The EFFECTIVE mode of the pair, which is the OR of both sides per-device settings (C-1). A computer advertises the OR of its own web and extension settings. Never read back from the server as truth.",
  "consumers": [
    "web page",
    "chrome-extension service worker",
    "Android app (instrumented SasVectorsTest, P4)"
  ],
